module bindings.javascript.parsedUrlHostnamePatternCheck;

// The WHATWG URL constructor is the documented way to take a string apart
// into the parts an outbound request is actually issued against, and
// `hostname` is the part a destination filter has to decide on: it is the
// name that will be resolved and connected to, with no userinfo, port or
// path to hide a target in. RegExp.prototype.test answers exactly one
// question about a string -- does it match this pattern -- so a function
// that parses a URL, reads the parsed hostname and applies a pattern to it
// has judged the destination the request will use, which is what an
// allowlist or a private-range filter consists of before the transport
// carries it out.
//
// The gate is the conjunction of all three readings, in one function. A
// hostname is read for a dozen unrelated reasons in ordinary code -- virtual
// host routing, link rewriting, logging -- and `.test` is the ordinary way
// to match any value, so neither reading alone says anything about a
// destination. The URL construction is what ties them together: it is the
// only reading in the set that produces a hostname worth validating rather
// than one already known. Function scope because the validation and the
// request it licenses are statements of one handler; a filter factored into
// a sibling function is a different shape, and the sibling's tokens do not
// reach the handler (a `new URL` in one function credits nothing in
// another).
//
// Residual, in two directions. The pattern itself is not classified: an
// anchored list of literal hosts and a private-range exclusion are the same
// three readings, and both are host filters, so both are credited -- the
// distinction between an allowlist and a denylist is not recoverable from a
// pattern reliably enough to gate on. And the reading is not tied to the
// value that is fetched: a handler that validates one URL and requests
// another in the same function is credited for both, the same function-wide
// credit a canonicalising path check carries.
binding jsParsedUrlHostnamePatternCheck {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "javascript" and node.context.call in ["URL"] and node.context.call in ["test"] and node.context.selector contains ".hostname"
  emit check core.AllowlistValidation at node {
    covers endpoint {
      anchor: node
    }
  }
}
